@AThousandShips Well, I found the issue. In the `_shape_changed` function inside `editor/scene/2d/physics/collision_shape_2d_editor_plugin.cpp` this line was changed from:
```cpp
if (current_shape.is_valid()) {
```
to
```cpp
if (current_shape.is_valid() && !EditorNode::get_singleton()->is_resource_read_only(current_shape)) {
```
which is fine, but the `is_resource_read_only` function which is located in the `editor/editor_node.cpp` file mistakenly returns true for a resource that is in an instantiated scene made editable before the scene is saved. Don't know if I'm qualified enough to touch this though.

> For example, in my actual project I have a custom `ResourceFormatLoader` I'll call `ExampleLoader` which loads a custom resource called `ExampleResource`. This `ExampleResource` has a method which allows me to create a custom node I'll call `ExamplePlayerNode` using the contents of the resource. This seems architecturally reasonable to me and works without issue. I've even seen a few Godot tutorials recommending something like this.

@Quantix
A very simple way around this is making `ExamplePlayerNode` have a static function which takes the resource as an argument. This way `ExampleResource`doesn't need to reference `ExamplePlayerNode`. Resources are supposed to *mostly* be agnostic and not reference Nodes. Godot's lax approach permits you to do whatever you want most of the time, with this being an exception. The more advanced features tend to have less guard rails.

